import { SITE_NAME } from "../lib/site";
---

<TextPage
  searchable
  title="How it works"
  description={`How ${SITE_NAME} collects used-electronics listings in Canada and calculates average asking prices, price cutoffs and deals.`}
  intro="Where the numbers come from, what counts, and how to read them."
>
  <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
  <p>
    We collect public used-electronics listings from Facebook Marketplace across Canada several times a day. Each listing's
    title, asking price, condition, city and date are stored. We show titles and prices and always link back to the original
    listing; we do not copy listing photos or descriptions.
  </p>

  <h2>Which listings count</h2>
  <p>Every listing is checked before it is used in any average. We only use <strong>comparable</strong> listings:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>the listing is for the model in question (not an accessory, a case or a different product),</li>
    <li>it has a price above zero,</li>
    <li>its condition is not "Poor" and the text does not mention a defect, and</li>
    <li>its price is between 0.3 and 3 times the model's typical (median) price, which removes joke prices and typing errors.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>All storage sizes and colours of a model are grouped together, so averages mix configurations.</p>

  <h2>The numbers explained</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>30-day average and 7-day average</dt>
    <dd>
      The average asking price of the comparable listings posted in the last 30 days and the last 7 days. The 7-day average
      reacts faster; the 30-day average is steadier.
    </dd>
    <dt>Cheapest 25% under</dt>
    <dd>
      The price that a quarter of the comparable listings of the last 30 days fall under. A listing priced below it is cheaper
      than about three quarters of the listings, which makes it a handy yardstick for "is this cheap?".
    </dd>
    <dt>vs avg</dt>
    <dd>A listing's price minus the 30-day average. A negative figure (green) means it is cheaper than average; positive (red) means pricier.</dd>
    <dt>High-scoring listings (deal score 8 or more)</dt>
    <dd>
      Each listing receives a deal score from an automated, AI-assisted classification step. Higher means a better deal for
      the buyer. We show separate figures for listings that score 8 or more. The score is an estimate, not a guarantee.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>How deals are ranked</h2>
  <p>
    On the Top Deals pages, the comparable listings of the last 30 days are ranked from the lowest price up and each price is
    compared with the 30-day average. Models need enough listings (at least ten) before deals are shown.
  </p>

  <h2>What to keep in mind</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>These are asking prices. Sellers often accept less, and some listings never sell.</li>
    <li>We do not verify that a listing is still available, and a very low price can mean damage, a scam or an item that is already gone.</li>
    <li>Listings are matched to models automatically, so occasional mistakes are possible.</li>
    <li>Times are shown in Toronto time. The last update is shown at the bottom of every page.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Questions or corrections</h2>
  <p>See the <a href="/contact/">Contact</a> page.</p>
</TextPage>
